---
title: Split .NET Tests Across Tasks
description: Run one Nx task per test class or test method so a slow .NET suite can be cached and distributed independently
sidebar:
  label: Split .NET Tests
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - '.NET'
---

A long .NET test suite runs as one task, so it caches as one unit and lands on one agent. Splitting
it gives you a task per test class, each cached separately and distributed across agents.

Turn it on by naming a CI target for a test project:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "plugins": [
    { "plugin": "@nx/dotnet" },
    {
      "plugin": "@nx/dotnet",
      "include": ["apps/IntegrationTests/**"],
      "options": { "test": { "ciTargetName": "test-ci" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

`ciTargetName` applies to every project the registration matches, so the second entry with an
`include` pattern is what scopes splitting to one project. Everything else keeps its ordinary `test`
target.

That project now has a `test-ci--<class>` target per test class, plus a `test-ci` target that
depends on all of them:

```shell
nx show project IntegrationTests
```

Run one on its own to see it work:

```shell
nx run IntegrationTests:"test-ci--Acme.Integration.CheckoutTests"
```

## Requirements

Your test project must use MSTest on [Microsoft.Testing
Platform](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/testing/microsoft-testing-platform-intro). Each
task selects its tests with `--filter`, whose expression syntax MSTest contributes to the platform.
Either use the `MSTest.Sdk` project SDK, or set these properties:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <EnableMSTestRunner>true</EnableMSTestRunner>
  <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>true</TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>
  <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
</PropertyGroup>
```

A project that asks to split without them gets a message explaining why, and keeps its ordinary
`test` target. Other frameworks on the platform are left alone rather than given tasks that would
fail when their runner rejects the option.

Running the whole group needs [Nx Cloud](/docs/getting-started/intro). Nx refuses `nx test-ci` without
it: on one machine you pay process startup for every task without gaining agents to spread them
over. Use the `test` target for a full local run.

Individual tasks have no such restriction — run one on its own anytime, the way you did above.

## Choose class or method

`ciSplitBy` defaults to `class`. One task per test class keeps the task count close to your file
count, and each task pays process startup once for a whole class of tests.

Switch to `method` when a single test method is expensive on its own:

```json
{
  "plugin": "@nx/dotnet",
  "include": ["apps/IntegrationTests/**"],
  "options": { "test": { "ciTargetName": "test-ci", "ciSplitBy": "method" } }
}
```

The case this exists for is a suite where each method builds and tears down its own fixture — a
distributed application, a database, a browser. Grouping those methods into one task saves no setup
work, since the fixture cost is paid per method either way, and it serializes tests that could have
run on separate agents. A method that takes minutes makes the extra second of process startup
irrelevant.

For a fast unit suite, `method` is the wrong trade — a task graph with thousands of entries, each
running a test measured in milliseconds.

## What still runs in one task

Nested and generic test classes are not split out, because their names have no stable command-line
form to filter on. Generic test methods are excluded for the same reason under `ciSplitBy: method`.
Those tests still run as part of the ordinary `test` target, and the plugin reports how many it left
out while building the project graph, so the gap is visible up front.

An abstract test class isn't split either, but that costs you nothing under `ciSplitBy: class` — the
concrete classes that derive from it are what carry tests, and each of those gets its own task
regardless of where the tests it runs are declared.

Method mode splits only the `[TestMethod]`s a class declares directly, so tests a subclass inherits
from a base get no task of their own, whether or not that subclass declares any itself. They still
run under `test`, just not in the split run.

A subclass declaring no test method of its own is counted and explained, but one that declares some
and inherits the rest loses the inherited ones silently. Use `ciSplitBy: class` if your suite leans
on inheritance. Class mode keeps the subclass as one task, wherever its tests are declared.

## Parallelism

Three things decide how many tests run at once, and they multiply:

| Level                                      | Controlled by                                              |
| ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| How many agents the tasks spread across    | Nx Cloud                                                   |
| How many tasks run at once on one agent    | `nx --parallel`, default 3                                 |
| How many tests run at once inside one task | MSTest `[assembly: Parallelize(Workers = N, Scope = ...)]` |

The plugin doesn't touch `Workers`. That stays your suite's lever, and it still applies to the
ordinary `test` target exactly as before.

What changes is how much it has to work with. Each split task is filtered down to one class or one
method, so `Scope = ClassLevel` has nothing left to parallelize once the filter has picked a single
class. `Scope = MethodLevel` still parallelizes that class's methods inside the task, which pairs
well with `ciSplitBy: class` — Nx spreads classes across agents while MSTest runs each class's
methods concurrently in one process, paying assembly setup once.

Watch the multiplication if your tests are resource-hungry. A 16-core agent with the default
`Workers = 0` and `--parallel 3` can have 48 tests in flight. Set `MSTestParallelizeWorkers` in the
`.csproj` to cap it per project:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <MSTestParallelizeWorkers>2</MSTestParallelizeWorkers>
</PropertyGroup>
```

For tests that must not overlap on one machine, `[DoNotParallelize]` on a class makes its task run
on its own — Nx still distributes it to an agent, it just won't share that agent with another task.

## Test reports

Each task writes into its own directory under `TestResults`, named for the class or method it ran,
so restoring one task from cache can't overwrite another's results.

The plugin doesn't enable a reporter. Pick one in the project so the split and unsplit targets
report the same way:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <TestingPlatformCommandLineArguments>--report-trx</TestingPlatformCommandLineArguments>
</PropertyGroup>
```

TRX output requires the `Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.TrxReport` package, which the `MSTest`
metapackage includes from version 3.8. Collect the results in CI with a glob, since there's no
merge step:

```shell
**/TestResults/**/*.trx
```

## Code coverage

Coverage is configured on the project and works the same whether or not you split, since the plugin
passes nothing that interferes with it. `MSTest.Sdk` bundles the coverage extension, so `--coverage`
is available without adding a package:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
  <TestingPlatformCommandLineArguments>--coverage --coverage-output-format cobertura</TestingPlatformCommandLineArguments>
</PropertyGroup>
```

What changes is that each task covers only the tests it ran, so you get one report per task instead
of one for the project. Merge them with `dotnet-coverage`, which ships from Microsoft:

```shell
dotnet tool install -g dotnet-coverage
dotnet-coverage merge "**/TestResults/**/*.cobertura.xml" -f cobertura -o coverage.cobertura.xml
```

The merged report is the union, not a sum of percentages. A class covering four of seven lines and
another covering two of the remaining three merge to six of seven.

## Verify the split covers everything

Every task fails if its filter matches no tests, which catches a class the plugin discovered but the
platform can't find. To check for the opposite problem — tests running twice — compare the total
across the split tasks against the unsplit run:

```shell
nx test IntegrationTests
nx test-ci IntegrationTests
```

The counts should match.
